#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179545 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "892f45fd-1e75-5500-90c3-0218fab5f1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Lines to Make Predictions"}] :question/id #uuid "1900f9ee-5295-5e56-b9d5-2dda8649b212" :question/math-academy-id "e-9684" :question/problem "The scatter plot above shows the distances some athletes in a sports club cover during a training session, and the time (in minutes) it takes. Use the trend line to estimate how long it takes a club athlete to run $10$ km.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3753/Source/Images/e5acbcf6efb94071c33cfaf698ee586e.png)" :question/worked-solution "To make our estimation, we proceed as follows:\n\n- We start at $y=10$ on the vertical axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3753/Source/Images/1ad00f5afd6b7df9461f68043fd06474.png)\n\n- Then, we move right until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3753/Source/Images/3b7b5b068bbf97919b87f2563268cfd0.png)\n\n- Finally, we move down until we intersect the horizontal axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3753/Source/Images/9966735dc779084081b8242b65932697.png)\n\nTherefore, a club athlete can run $10$ kilometers in approximately $45$ minutes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179546 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "877509f5-a5de-55d2-ab5d-6c37fc5b0650" :knowledge-point/title "Making a Prediction by Estimating a Trend Line"}] :question/id #uuid "2bc871c4-b557-5cff-8ab2-55d9cb6e24fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-9690" :question/problem "The scatter plot shows the value of an artwork, in thousands of dollars, several decades after it was created. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the time it takes for the work of art to be worth $\\[math]5\\,000$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3753/Source/Images/c7145faa85aff583b7414d72979aed4c.png)\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(1,1.5)$ and $(4,3)$.*" :question/worked-solution "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3753/Source/Images/42a7269d76cd0018b398919003437c40.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = (1,1.5)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2,y_2) = (4,3)\n$$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 - 1.5}{4 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1.5}{3} \\\\\n&= 0.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m = 0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny = 0.5x + b\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b$. We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 0.5x + b \\\\\n3 &= 0.5(4) + b \\\\\n3 &= 2 + b \\\\\nb &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny = 0.5x + 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y = 5$ into the equation above and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 0.5x + 1 \\\\\n5 &= 0.5x + 1 \\\\\n0.5x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the artwork will be worth $\\[math]5\\,000$ approximately $8$ decades after it was created.\n\n**Note:** The point where $y=5$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179547 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28adc95e-e6e5-580c-9eed-55a3d12d5232" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Line Equations to Make a Prediction"}] :question/id #uuid "3a864a65-e753-5bd5-b4fd-b3e95fc5a2de" :question/math-academy-id "e-9692" :question/problem "The scatter plot above shows the relationship between a book’s spine thickness (in millimetres) and the total number of pages for a random sample of children's books. The equation of the trend line is $y = 10x$. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the thickness of a book with $200$ pages.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3753/Source/Images/9ea29d6f32c24c53ff7138e53fa27e68.png)" :question/worked-solution "To predict the thickness in a book with $200$ pages, we need to substitute $y = 200$ into the equation of the trend line and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 10x \\\\\n200 &= 10x \\\\\n\\frac{200}{10} &= x \\\\\n20 &= x \\\\\nx &= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, a book with $200$ pages should have approximately a thickness of $20\\text{mm}$.\n\n**Note:** The point where $y=200$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "892f45fd-1e75-5500-90c3-0218fab5f1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Lines to Make Predictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83dad1b3-13a4-598d-b103-a011fc453713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2006" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177971} {:answer/id #uuid "01fbee12-0a98-5a04-a76d-8fff3ddefdc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177972} {:answer/id #uuid "2556dbd8-0c0a-5469-8ba2-5b2917fa869d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2010" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177973} {:answer/id #uuid "3dbcef0f-5cad-5f1a-9861-ec095aa54bf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177974} {:answer/id #uuid "412581cb-60af-50c7-ad2f-36832d96ae9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2004" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2556dbd8-0c0a-5469-8ba2-5b2917fa869d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2010" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177973} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1a89c83-5249-5b02-913e-9446